\title{Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make Vision-Language-Action Models Safer?\\ An Empirical Investigation}

\author{
Anonymous Author(s)
}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Fine-tuned Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models can achieve high task success yet remain behaviorally unsafe, with collision avoidance rates substantially lagging task completion on safety-critical benchmarks.
We present a systematic factorial study isolating how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance, with twelve conditions varying gradient flow, label content, normalization type, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent.
Our central finding is negative: \textbf{all auxiliary spatial head configurations perform at or below the no-auxiliary-head baseline} on Collision Avoidance Rate (0 of 12 configurations improve; sign test on four factorial cell means, $p{=}0.0625$, with directional consistency across all twelve structurally related conditions). A $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) shows a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow through the auxiliary head into the vision encoders is associated with lower safety under both normalization types ($\Delta{=}-4.2$\,pp within BatchNorm, $-$2.5\,pp within LayerNorm). Effect sizes are large but the small-sample design ($n{\leq}4$ per cell) limits pairwise power; whether normalization type modulates the magnitude requires additional LN seeds to confirm. The BatchNorm + gradient flow cell is both the worst-performing and the most unstable configuration, with cross-seed standard deviation more than $2.5{\times}$ that of other cells.
Gradient analysis confirms orthogonality between action and auxiliary gradients (cosine similarity near zero), ruling out direct gradient conflict. Spatial label content (ground-truth occupancy, random binary labels, egocentric depth maps) does not affect safety outcomes, indicating that the gradient pathway, not the spatial information, determines the result.
Practical guidelines: do not add auxiliary spatial heads for safety purposes. If an auxiliary head is required by other objectives, use detached gradients with LayerNorm (Detach+LN), and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow. The auxiliary head is discarded after training at zero inference cost.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Vision-Language-Action Models, Robot Safety, Auxiliary Supervision, Collision Avoidance}

